WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
remember resident evil 4's original  gamecube case the one that said only  four in the corner it's hard to imagine  now considering the game has been ported  every which way to the nokia phone and  back but despite its acclaimed status  all its ports and the modest visual  facelift it has received over the years  one thing remains woefully unchanged and  firmly cements resident evil 4 in 2005  when it was originally released those  damned tank controls even the wii  edition the best port of the game i must  say was shackled by its rigid movement  that is until resident evil 4 vr which  presents the game in its best light  since its original release playing  through the action horror classic on the  oculus quest 2 has given me a feeling  i've wanted since first finishing  resident evil 4 16 years ago on the  gamecube the sense of experiencing it  for the first time all over again and  that feeling is downright  awesome  from the ground up the game has been  meticulously recreated in unreal engine  4 in uncanny detail to fully function in  vr and the result is so impressive it's  nothing short of magic from upscale  textures to sound effects and animations  resinual 4 can now be seen quite  literally through an entirely new lens  stepping into the shoes of protagonist  and pretty boy leon s kennedy on a  mission to save the president's daughter  has never felt cooler nor has the horror  representation of eastern europe ever  felt more  frightening nearly everything has been  updated to accommodate vr your equipment  is now accessible on your physical body  shoulder for your knife chest for a  grenade and utility belt for weapon  magazines this means physically loading  your gun and cocking it before firing it  off unsheathing your knife to break open  crates or pulling the pin on a grenade  and letting it cook before tossing at  the feet of some gennados and performing  nearly every action made me feel like a  total president-daughter-saving badass  vr has unanimously excelled at first  person combat and it shines  exceptionally well in resident evil 4 vr  shooting dynamite out of someone's hands  juking out of the way of a thrown  hatchet unloading a submachine gun with  one hand and pulling out a pistol with  the other to show off enemies next me  felt so  satisfying  even just opening cabinets was an oddly  thrilling new way to interact with a  world i know so well pumping a shotgun  and squeezing the trigger point-blank in  an enemy's face followed with a  blood-drenched explosion of their skull  only for tentacles to erupt from its  neck up close and personal in vr is gasp  inducing and even when i was shaken with  anxiety there is no denying i had a  smile from ear to ear while combat is  far more versatile in ways the original  never could be like duel wielding a  knife and a pistol or even a shotgun and  a submachine gun don't be fooled the  odds still stagged against me and each  onslaught of gonados felt harrowing  resident evil 4 vr still manages to  capture the tension and stress of the  original very well and honestly dual  wielding and guns blazing is rarely a  route worth taking as it can be a waste  of resources but there's no denying it  got me out of some dire situations as  mentioned actions that were once a  simple button press now require  physicality from the player like opening  cabinets drawers toggling switches  turning door keys and solving puzzles  it's a little touch and not an entirely  remarkable idea for a vr title but it  remains effective in elevating the  residential 4 experience being able to  open drawers or slowly ease a door to  see what's around the corner has a huge  impact on immersion and helps the game  feel like a new and modern experience  even saving on a typewriter now involves  physically pressing the mechanical keys  of an early 20th century typewriter and  it is such a simple delight some things  are locked to button presses and  rightfully so like jumping out of a  window or hopping over a barrier and in  these cases it switches the third person  for me someone who experiences minor  motion sickness i was grateful for this  but your mileage may vary you can also  opt to remain in first person thanks to  an option found in the settings seeing  resident evil 4's iconic enemies and  environments up close emphasizes the  feeling of experiencing it for the first  time again i was able to notice detail  before like enegonado's face its eyes  vacant and googly with their mouth  slightly ajar and crooked it made an  already unsettling enemy feel far more  eerie and intrusive environments i had  seen a dozen times before in my previous  console playthroughs felt all the more  engrossing it took more time to observe  the haunting victorian paintings in the  castle or that cart filled with corpses  by the farm just looking at the dirty  water in the well as i reached into it  to pick up treasure made me want to wash  my hands in real life as for  accessibility the options are plentiful  it ranges from full motion to standing  sitting down and whether you're right or  left-handed there's also different modes  of interaction such as immersive which  puts all the gear and weapons on your  physical body or comfort mode where your  arsenal can be switched and rotated  through with a few button presses  additionally there's a wealth of options  to tweak and finesse your ideal playing  experience i played in immersive mode  standing with teleportation movement for  the entirety of my 13 hour playthrough  and rarely did i experience any  discomfort but not everything works as  well as it's intended counter moves in  particular is a mechanic i abandoned  using early in my playthrough counter  moves like kicks and supluxes can be  performed after stunning an enemy by  shooting them in the knees or in the  face in these occurrences i found myself  clumsily fumbling to get close enough to  an enemy to press the button and  initiate it often completely missing the  window of chance and thrusting myself  into harm's way unintentionally the  action felt clumsy and unintuitive  compared to the physical motion of  everything else so i eventually just  stopped using it some boss fights also  don't translate smoothly into vr del  lago the swamp boss fight in particular  proved to be a point of frustration for  me controlling the boat while being  pulled along by the giant beast and  having to simultaneously load a harpoon  gun and fire it was a lot to manage it's  something that worked well on console  but having the full motion of vr while  operating multiple things at once was a  little too disorienting to my surprise  however parts i dreaded returning to  from the original specifically the  game's final action focus act were  actually improved thanks to vr  traditionally considering ra4's original  rigid movements and lack of cover system  fighting off the game's final military  armed foes was usually a slow slog to  run around a corner only to get a shot  in before scurrying away pacing the  original game became disjointed and kind  of out of character in these moments but  in vr the action is amplified thanks to  being able to physically duck around  corners take cover shoot through  openings in the wall or toss grenades  out it felt amazing and made getting in  a shootout with crossbow wielding  gatling guns shooting axe throwing goons  a total blast  this exhilarating implementation of  combat makes the lack of mercenaries  mode however all the more apparent  playing re4's action segments through vr  lenses left me wanting to see how its  action horror can translate in the  arcade style experience of mercenary so  its absence is a minor letdown so  instead the moment the credits rolled i  began my second playthrough on new game  plus with plans to run through an  additional harder professional  difficulty mode there's no game i've  completed more times in my life than  residing before nor is there a game i've  purchased as many times but experiencing  it via a different format is an  astonishing experience bringing the 16  year old classic to vr doesn't just  reaffirm why it's a classic to begin  with but is now among the best vr games  on offer and it's evident that building  the game from the ground up was a true  labor of love residual 4 vr is an  essential game to have in your oculus  quest 2 library right alongside beat  saber and super hot it is a welcoming  version to newcomers that are vr capable  and most excitingly not only will  players get to experience resume 4 in a  new way that doesn't compromise the  original's greatness even with its  quarks but a new generation of players  will get to hear leon s kennedy's campy  one-liners and that's a prospect  i'm very excited about  [Music]  [Music]  foreign

